1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,475
Munshi Abdullah: The Malay Scribe
Who Watched Singapore Begin

2
00:00:04,475 --> 00:00:08,112
One evening in March 1823,
a young Malay teacher on

3
00:00:08,192 --> 00:00:12,120
his way to give a language
lesson met William Farquhar,

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,837
the British Resident of
Singapore, coming the other

5
00:00:15,917 --> 00:00:19,925
way with his son, his son-in-law
and four armed sepoys.

6
00:00:19,925 --> 00:00:23,961
When Farquhar heard where he was
going, he told him to turn back

7
00:00:24,041 --> 00:00:28,350
and come along, because a man had
run amok near a merchant's house.

8
00:00:28,350 --> 00:00:31,496
Within the hour the teacher
was holding Farquhar

9
00:00:31,576 --> 00:00:34,600
up as he collapsed, his
coat soaked in blood.

10
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:38,972
The teacher was Abdullah bin Abdul
Kadir, known as Munshi Abdullah,

11
00:00:39,052 --> 00:00:42,095
and twenty years later
he wrote the whole night

12
00:00:42,175 --> 00:00:45,749
down in the Hikayat Abdullah,
one of the few first-hand

13
00:00:45,829 --> 00:00:49,550
accounts of Singapore's
founding years written in Malay.

14
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:52,325
The trouble began with a debt.

15
00:00:52,325 --> 00:00:55,000
Abdullah tells it this way.

16
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,495
A trader from Pahang named Syed
Yassin owed about 1,400 dollars,

17
00:01:00,575 --> 00:01:05,975
most of it to Pangeran Sharif
Omar, a merchant from Palembang.

18
00:01:05,975 --> 00:01:09,248
Farquhar heard the case,
and when Syed Yassin said

19
00:01:09,328 --> 00:01:12,667
he could not pay until the
next year and could find

20
00:01:12,747 --> 00:01:16,100
no one to guarantee the
debt, he was sent to jail.

21
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:20,575
Nobody searched him, and he had
a kris hidden under his coat.

22
00:01:20,575 --> 00:01:24,338
That evening he asked to be
allowed to see the Pangeran,

23
00:01:24,418 --> 00:01:27,564
to plead for more time,
and he was let out with

24
00:01:27,644 --> 00:01:31,075
an Indian peon, a police
constable, as his escort.

25
00:01:31,075 --> 00:01:34,266
When the Pangeran saw him
coming, he ran out of the

26
00:01:34,346 --> 00:01:37,425
back of his house and
went to Farquhar for help.

27
00:01:37,425 --> 00:01:40,814
Syed Yassin stabbed the
peon dead at the gate and

28
00:01:40,894 --> 00:01:44,575
hid in the dark garden,
under a raised summer-house.

29
00:01:44,575 --> 00:01:48,850
Farquhar searched the grounds
three times and found no one.

30
00:01:48,850 --> 00:01:51,500
As the party walked back across the lawn,

31
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:54,975
he pushed his walking stick
under the summer-house.

32
00:01:54,975 --> 00:01:57,681
Syed Yassin lunged out and stabbed him

33
00:01:57,761 --> 00:02:00,400
in the chest, just above the nipple.

34
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:04,300
Farquhar cried out, and
Abdullah caught him as he fell.

35
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:08,050
Farquhar's son Andrew slashed
the attacker across the face

36
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:12,225
with his sword, and the sepoys
killed him with their bayonets.

37
00:02:12,225 --> 00:02:15,225
The wound turned out to be shallow.

38
00:02:15,225 --> 00:02:18,400
The doctor probed it with
a silver needle and told

39
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:22,125
Farquhar's weeping daughters
that he would soon be well.

40
00:02:22,125 --> 00:02:25,675
Outside, though, the
settlement was in uproar.

41
00:02:25,675 --> 00:02:29,493
Hundreds of sepoys, some only
half dressed, dragged up twelve

42
00:02:29,573 --> 00:02:34,158
cannon and aimed them at the compound
of the Temenggong, the Malay chief,

43
00:02:34,238 --> 00:02:37,625
because at first the British
suspected his followers.

44
00:02:37,625 --> 00:02:40,667
Stamford Raffles, who
was then in Singapore,

45
00:02:40,747 --> 00:02:44,650
arrived in his carriage and
asked who the dead man was.

46
00:02:44,650 --> 00:02:47,108
Nobody could tell, because the body

47
00:02:47,188 --> 00:02:49,725
had been hacked beyond recognition.

48
00:02:49,725 --> 00:02:53,112
When it came out that the
officer who had taken Syed

49
00:02:53,192 --> 00:02:56,045
Yassin to jail had let
him out under escort,

50
00:02:56,125 --> 00:03:00,325
Raffles shook his fist in the
man's face, knocking off his hat.

51
00:03:00,325 --> 00:03:04,043
In the 1874 English
translation of the Hikayat,

52
00:03:04,123 --> 00:03:09,700
Raffles tells him, "if Farquhar
dies, I shall hang you in Singapore."

53
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:14,230
The next morning, Raffles asked
Sultan Hussein and the Temenggong

54
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:19,275
what Malay custom laid down for a
man who drew the blood of his ruler.

55
00:03:19,275 --> 00:03:23,508
They told him that the man, his
wives and his children would all

56
00:03:23,588 --> 00:03:27,900
be put to death and his house
torn down and thrown into the sea.

57
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:32,325
Raffles refused to punish the
family, who had done nothing.

58
00:03:32,325 --> 00:03:36,164
Instead, the body was drawn
around the town in a buffalo

59
00:03:36,244 --> 00:03:39,802
cart to the beat of a gong,
and then hung in an iron

60
00:03:39,882 --> 00:03:43,301
cage on a mast at Tanjong
Malang, near Telok Ayer,

61
00:03:43,381 --> 00:03:47,300
for ten or fifteen days, until
only the bones were left.

62
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:51,625
Only then was it handed to
the Sultan to be buried.

63
00:03:51,625 --> 00:03:56,300
Abdullah was born in 1797
in Kampong Pali, Malacca,

64
00:03:56,380 --> 00:04:00,575
the only one of five
sons to survive infancy.

65
00:04:00,575 --> 00:04:04,360
His father, Sheikh Abdul
Kadir, was a devout Muslim of

66
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:08,655
Arab and Indian descent who ran
a petition-writing business,

67
00:04:08,735 --> 00:04:11,733
and he drilled his son
hard: the boy copied

68
00:04:11,813 --> 00:04:15,750
the whole Koran in Arabic
and translated it into Malay.

69
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:19,170
While still a boy, Abdullah
taught religion to Muslim

70
00:04:19,250 --> 00:04:22,010
soldiers of the Indian
garrison at Malacca,

71
00:04:22,090 --> 00:04:26,250
and they gave him the name that
stuck for the rest of his life.

72
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:28,650
"Munshi" means teacher.

73
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:32,920
In December 1810, Raffles
arrived in Malacca to prepare

74
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,903
the British invasion of Java and
hired Abdullah, then about 13,

75
00:04:37,983 --> 00:04:42,175
as the youngest of his
copyists of Malay manuscripts.

76
00:04:42,175 --> 00:04:47,192
When Raffles left for Java the next year,
he wanted to take Abdullah with him,

77
00:04:47,272 --> 00:04:50,800
but Abdullah's mother would
not let her only child go.

78
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:55,545
From 1815 he taught Malay to
Protestant missionaries in Malacca,

79
00:04:55,625 --> 00:05:00,294
and some time after June 1819 he
left for the new settlement at

80
00:05:00,374 --> 00:05:05,425
Singapore with one of them, the
German missionary Claudius Thomsen.

81
00:05:05,425 --> 00:05:08,518
There he taught Malay
and worked for a time

82
00:05:08,598 --> 00:05:11,475
as Raffles's secretary and interpreter.

83
00:05:11,475 --> 00:05:15,493
Abdullah's Singapore was a
small town cut out of forest,

84
00:05:15,573 --> 00:05:19,525
and some of his liveliest
pages are about the animals.

85
00:05:19,525 --> 00:05:23,425
Rats were everywhere, some
almost the size of cats.

86
00:05:23,425 --> 00:05:28,155
One night he woke to his cat
screaming and lit a torch to find six

87
00:05:28,235 --> 00:05:32,900
or seven rats holding it down by
the ears, the legs and the jaw.

88
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:36,325
It took neighbours with
sticks to beat them off.

89
00:05:36,325 --> 00:05:40,575
Farquhar's answer, as Abdullah
tells it, was a bounty.

90
00:05:40,575 --> 00:05:44,669
He paid a small sum for every
dead rat brought to his house,

91
00:05:44,749 --> 00:05:47,313
and at first people came with almost a

92
00:05:47,393 --> 00:05:51,150
thousand a morning, some
carrying fifty or sixty each.

93
00:05:51,150 --> 00:05:55,101
When the rats kept coming, he
cut the reward to five duit,

94
00:05:55,181 --> 00:05:58,228
a small copper coin,
had a deep trench dug to

95
00:05:58,308 --> 00:06:01,912
bury the bodies, and in time
only ten or twenty a day

96
00:06:01,992 --> 00:06:05,675
were being handed in, until
the infestation was over.

97
00:06:05,675 --> 00:06:09,470
He did the same for the
centipedes that were biting people

98
00:06:09,550 --> 00:06:13,425
in their houses, and Abdullah
says it worked just as well.

99
00:06:13,425 --> 00:06:18,410
On another occasion, a crocodile in
the Rochor River took Farquhar's dog,

100
00:06:18,490 --> 00:06:22,435
and when the animal was cornered
and speared, its body was

101
00:06:22,515 --> 00:06:27,025
hung from a tree beside the Bras
Basah river for everyone to see.

102
00:06:27,025 --> 00:06:30,632
Abdullah knew Raffles well,
and his description of

103
00:06:30,712 --> 00:06:34,400
him is more personal than
most official portraits.

104
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:37,692
He saw a man of middle
height with a broad forehead,

105
00:06:37,772 --> 00:06:40,157
fair hair, thick eyebrows and a slight

106
00:06:40,237 --> 00:06:43,350
squint in his left eye,
who walked with a stoop.

107
00:06:43,350 --> 00:06:47,853
Raffles was courteous to everyone,
he wrote, and "spoke in smiles",

108
00:06:47,933 --> 00:06:51,273
but he kept to a strict
timetable and would see no

109
00:06:51,353 --> 00:06:55,525
one until he had finished whatever
he was reading or writing.

110
00:06:55,525 --> 00:06:59,284
In the evenings Raffles would
lie back with his eyes closed,

111
00:06:59,364 --> 00:07:03,571
so still that Abdullah often thought
he was asleep, then spring up,

112
00:07:03,651 --> 00:07:08,450
write for a while and lie down again,
until midnight or one in the morning.

113
00:07:08,450 --> 00:07:11,690
The next day he would read
over what he had written,

114
00:07:11,770 --> 00:07:16,175
pacing up and down, and keep perhaps
three or four sheets out of ten.

115
00:07:16,175 --> 00:07:19,104
He paid four men to
collect plants, insects,

116
00:07:19,184 --> 00:07:24,371
shells and animals, kept a barrel of
arrack to preserve snakes and scorpions,

117
00:07:24,451 --> 00:07:27,050
and bought Malay books by the hundred.

118
00:07:27,050 --> 00:07:31,009
Abdullah noted that so many
manuscripts were sold to him

119
00:07:31,089 --> 00:07:35,200
that Malacca's store of old
histories was nearly used up.

120
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:39,772
When the British cleared the point
at the mouth of the Singapore River,

121
00:07:39,852 --> 00:07:42,720
workers came across a
large slab of sandstone

122
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:46,600
covered in carved letters worn
down by centuries of tides.

123
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:51,525
Abdullah describes crowds of every
community coming to look at it.

124
00:07:51,525 --> 00:07:55,045
The Hindus said the writing
was Hindu and the Chinese

125
00:07:55,125 --> 00:07:58,250
said it was Chinese, but
no one could read it.

126
00:07:58,250 --> 00:08:01,379
When Raffles took Abdullah
and Thomsen to see it,

127
00:08:01,459 --> 00:08:04,275
Abdullah thought the
letters looked Arabic.

128
00:08:04,275 --> 00:08:08,573
People pressed paste into the
grooves to take moulds and smeared

129
00:08:08,653 --> 00:08:11,925
the stone with ink to make
the letters stand out,

130
00:08:12,005 --> 00:08:16,725
and in the end Raffles concluded
that the script was Hindu in origin.

131
00:08:16,725 --> 00:08:20,750
The stone stayed where it was
for more than twenty years.

132
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:24,376
Around January 1843, it
was blown up on the orders

133
00:08:24,456 --> 00:08:26,896
of the acting settlement engineer,

134
00:08:26,976 --> 00:08:31,275
to widen the river mouth and
make room for Fort Fullerton.

135
00:08:31,275 --> 00:08:35,127
A few fragments were rescued
and sent to Calcutta,

136
00:08:35,207 --> 00:08:38,509
one of them came back
to Singapore in 1918,

137
00:08:38,589 --> 00:08:43,150
and that piece is now in the
National Museum of Singapore.

138
00:08:43,150 --> 00:08:45,766
Its script, possibly Old Javanese,

139
00:08:45,846 --> 00:08:48,700
has still not been fully deciphered.

140
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:51,642
Abdullah's book also records something the

141
00:08:51,722 --> 00:08:55,175
settlement's official
records said little about.

142
00:08:55,175 --> 00:08:59,266
During one Bugis trading season
he saw fifty or sixty slaves,

143
00:08:59,346 --> 00:09:03,505
men, women and children, driven
through the town "like sheep",

144
00:09:03,585 --> 00:09:06,308
and was told that a boat that had arrived

145
00:09:06,388 --> 00:09:09,875
the night before carried
two or three hundred more.

146
00:09:09,875 --> 00:09:13,150
Out of curiosity he
asked the price and was

147
00:09:13,230 --> 00:09:16,350
quoted thirty or forty dollars a person.

148
00:09:16,350 --> 00:09:19,525
The next day he rowed out to the boat.

149
00:09:19,525 --> 00:09:24,383
He found it packed with people, among
them pregnant women and young girls,

150
00:09:24,463 --> 00:09:27,800
and men tied by the waist
to the side of the boat.

151
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:30,562
He watched children cry
as their mothers were

152
00:09:30,642 --> 00:09:33,466
sold to different buyers,
and he wrote that he

153
00:09:33,546 --> 00:09:36,325
was ashamed to describe
some of what he saw.

154
00:09:36,325 --> 00:09:40,253
When he told Raffles, Raffles
said that the trade would not

155
00:09:40,333 --> 00:09:44,126
last much longer, because
Britain was turning against it,

156
00:09:44,206 --> 00:09:48,350
and added, "If I live, I hope
to see every slave a free man."

157
00:09:48,350 --> 00:09:52,569
The regulations Raffles
drew up for Singapore before

158
00:09:52,649 --> 00:09:56,700
he left in 1823 banned
slavery in the settlement.

159
00:09:56,700 --> 00:10:02,041
Raffles left Singapore in June 1823,
after falling out with Farquhar,

160
00:10:02,121 --> 00:10:05,500
whom he had dismissed
as Resident that May.

161
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:08,982
Abdullah wrote that when
he heard Raffles was going,

162
00:10:09,062 --> 00:10:12,350
he felt as if he had lost
his father and mother.

163
00:10:12,350 --> 00:10:16,288
Raffles wiped his own eyes,
told him he hoped to come back,

164
00:10:16,368 --> 00:10:21,475
and gave him a written testimonial so
that other Europeans would trust him.

165
00:10:21,475 --> 00:10:24,225
On board the ship, Lady Raffles gave him

166
00:10:24,305 --> 00:10:27,700
twenty-five dollars for
his children in Malacca.

167
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:29,723
As his small boat pulled away,

168
00:10:29,803 --> 00:10:33,369
Abdullah turned and saw
Raffles at the cabin window,

169
00:10:33,449 --> 00:10:37,375
and Raffles raised his hand
to him as the sails went up.

170
00:10:37,375 --> 00:10:42,525
Raffles never returned, and he
died in England three years later.

171
00:10:42,525 --> 00:10:45,725
Farquhar followed at the end of the year.

172
00:10:45,725 --> 00:10:50,289
Years later, Abdullah lost the
testimonial in a great fire near his

173
00:10:50,369 --> 00:10:55,625
house in the merchants' quarter, which
broke out while he lay ill with fever,

174
00:10:55,705 --> 00:10:59,725
along with his books, his
letters and 150 dollars in cash.

175
00:10:59,725 --> 00:11:03,609
Abdullah is a valuable
witness, but he was writing

176
00:11:03,689 --> 00:11:08,603
between 1840 and 1843, twenty
years after most of these events,

177
00:11:08,683 --> 00:11:11,775
and he was not present for all of them.

178
00:11:11,775 --> 00:11:15,936
His account of the first
British landing in 1819 was put

179
00:11:16,016 --> 00:11:20,555
together from what others told
him, and NLB's Infopedia notes

180
00:11:20,635 --> 00:11:24,800
that it fails to mention that
Raffles was there at all.

181
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:27,896
He also blamed the
destruction of the Singapore

182
00:11:27,976 --> 00:11:31,274
Stone on George Coleman,
the government architect,

183
00:11:31,354 --> 00:11:34,800
although Coleman was on
leave when it was blown up.

184
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:37,760
He was openly fond of Raffles, and he was

185
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:41,400
sharply critical of the
Malay rulers of his day.

186
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:44,899
Later readers have weighed
both against his closeness

187
00:11:44,979 --> 00:11:48,625
to the British, who employed
him for most of his life.

188
00:11:48,625 --> 00:11:51,750
The English version matters too.

189
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:57,086
The quotations here come from the
1874 translation by J. T. Thomson,

190
00:11:57,166 --> 00:12:00,511
the surveyor who had
been Abdullah's pupil,

191
00:12:00,591 --> 00:12:05,450
which Infopedia describes as
containing notable inaccuracies.

192
00:12:05,450 --> 00:12:09,106
The details here have
been checked against NLB's

193
00:12:09,186 --> 00:12:13,000
summaries of the later
translation by A. H. Hill.

194
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,638
Where his account can be
checked against other sources,

195
00:12:16,718 --> 00:12:20,300
as with the attack on
Farquhar, the main facts agree.

196
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:24,154
Once Raffles and Farquhar
had gone, Abdullah earned his

197
00:12:24,234 --> 00:12:28,025
living as a petition-writer,
interpreter and teacher.

198
00:12:28,025 --> 00:12:32,152
He taught Malay to merchants
such as Edward Boustead and,

199
00:12:32,232 --> 00:12:37,325
in the late 1830s, taught at the
Singapore Institution's free school.

200
00:12:37,325 --> 00:12:40,509
There he met the American
missionary Alfred North,

201
00:12:40,589 --> 00:12:44,882
who encouraged him to write his
life story, and Benjamin Keasberry,

202
00:12:44,963 --> 00:12:47,900
with whom he printed a
large number of books.

203
00:12:47,900 --> 00:12:51,563
He began the Hikayat Abdullah in 1840,

204
00:12:51,643 --> 00:12:55,208
finished the first draft in May 1843,

205
00:12:55,288 --> 00:12:59,425
and saw it printed in Jawi script in 1849.

206
00:12:59,425 --> 00:13:03,770
He also wrote an account
of a voyage to Kelantan

207
00:13:03,850 --> 00:13:08,275
and other northern Malay
states in 1837 or 1838.

208
00:13:08,275 --> 00:13:12,307
In 1854 he set out on
the pilgrimage to Mecca,

209
00:13:12,387 --> 00:13:14,800
keeping a diary as he went.

210
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:17,469
He died in October that year in Jeddah,

211
00:13:17,549 --> 00:13:21,425
according to Infopedia, though
some accounts say Mecca.

212
00:13:21,425 --> 00:13:24,675
The diary was published after his death.

213
00:13:24,675 --> 00:13:28,378
He is often called the father
of modern Malay literature,

214
00:13:28,458 --> 00:13:33,819
because he broke with the old court style
of legends and kings and wrote in plain,

215
00:13:33,899 --> 00:13:37,350
everyday Malay about things
he had seen for himself.

216
00:13:37,350 --> 00:13:40,306
Until the 1970s the Hikayat was a

217
00:13:40,386 --> 00:13:43,975
textbook in Malay schools in Singapore.

218
00:13:43,975 --> 00:13:47,897
Munshi Abdullah Avenue in
Singapore is named after him,

219
00:13:47,977 --> 00:13:52,627
and in 2019 he was one of the
pioneers whose statues stood beside

220
00:13:52,707 --> 00:13:56,629
Raffles's at the river for
the bicentennial, and one of

221
00:13:56,709 --> 00:14:01,075
the eight figures on the back
of the commemorative $20 note.

222
00:14:01,075 --> 00:14:05,604
Why it matters today: Most of what
is known about Singapore's first

223
00:14:05,684 --> 00:14:09,950
years comes from the letters and
reports of British officials.

224
00:14:09,950 --> 00:14:14,567
Abdullah's book is a rare view of the
same events from a local resident,

225
00:14:14,647 --> 00:14:18,300
who knew the officials but
saw the town from the street.

226
00:14:18,300 --> 00:14:21,461
It is not a neutral
record, and it should be

227
00:14:21,541 --> 00:14:25,150
read with the same care
as Raffles's own letters.

228
00:14:25,150 --> 00:14:28,972
But without it, much of everyday
life in early Singapore,

229
00:14:29,052 --> 00:14:33,775
from the rat bounty to the slave
boats, would be far less well known.
